There are two consumers, two states \begin_inset Formula $A,B$\end_inset
, and two goods in each state. Both consumers have equal state probabilities and state utility \begin_inset Formula $\log c_1+\log c_2$\end_inset
. Each owns \begin_inset Formula $\omega>0$\end_inset
 units of good 2 in each state. Consumer 1's good-1 endowment is \begin_inset Formula $(\omega/2,3\omega/2)$\end_inset
 across states, and consumer 2's is reversed. Good 1 is the state numeraire and \begin_inset Formula $r_s$\end_inset
 is the state-\begin_inset Formula $s$\end_inset
 price of good 2. Arrow security \begin_inset Formula $s$\end_inset
 pays one unit of good 1 in state \begin_inset Formula $s$\end_inset
. Consumer \begin_inset Formula $i$\end_inset
 holds \begin_inset Formula $\theta_{is}$\end_inset
, securities are in zero net supply, and the date-0 asset budget is \begin_inset Formula $Q_A\theta_{iA}+Q_B\theta_{iB}=0$\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Exercise*
\series bold
Part I -- by hand.\series default
 Normalize the state-\begin_inset Formula $B$\end_inset
 security price to \begin_inset Formula $Q_B=1$\end_inset
 and write \begin_inset Formula $q=Q_A$\end_inset
. Derive individual security demands as functions of \begin_inset Formula $q,r_A,r_B$\end_inset
. Show that commodity clearing under equal spot prices requires \begin_inset Formula $r_A=r_B=1$\end_inset
 and that the equilibrium security-price ratio is one. Calculate portfolios and state consumption, and verify both commodity and security clearing. Explain the role of strict concavity and price normalization in the uniqueness statement.
\end_layout

